  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 AM EDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nToday-Wednesday...Mid/upper level low that has lingered across the \narea finally weakens as it lifts northeast today. Pressure gradient \nremains weak across the area with winds light and variable each \nmorning, becoming onshore in the afternoon around 5-10 mph as sea \nbreeze develops and moves inland. Moist airmass remains in place, \nwith PW values around 2 inches. This will continue to lead to \nscattered to numerous showers and storms through midweek, mainly \ninto the afternoon and early evening hours with the inland moving \nsea breeze boundary. Rain chances range from 40-60% today and rise \nslightly to 50-70% on Wednesday. Temperatures begin to warm aloft as \nlow aloft breaks down and lifts away from the region, and the fairly \nsaturated airmass keeps DCAPE values relatively low (around 500-700 \nJ/kg). However, can't completely rule out isolated strong storms \neach afternoon. Primary threat looks to be locally heavy rainfall of \n1-3\" due to weak steering flow, which may lead to minor flooding of \nroadways and poor drainage areas. However, gusty winds to 40-50 mph \nand frequent lightning will be possible with any stronger \nconvection.  \n\nTemperatures will remain near to just slightly above normal, with \nhighs in the upper 80s to low 90s. Humid conditions will produce \npeak heat index values up to 100-103F each afternoon ahead of \ndeveloping storms. Warm and muggy conditions are forecast each \nnight, with lows in the low to mid 70s.  \n\nThursday-Friday...Strengthening low pressure offshore the Mid \nAtlantic coast lifts northward through late week, as mid/upper level \ntrough digs southward across the eastern U.S coast. This will push a \ncool front through the region into Thursday, with winds becoming \nbreezy to windy out of the N/NE each day into late week. Sustained \nspeeds up to 15-25 mph will be possible, with gusts up to 25-35 mph. \nStrongest wind speeds and gusts currently forecast to be focused \nalong the Volusia and Brevard County coastlines. The increase in\nwinds will lead to hazardous surf and boating conditions\ndeveloping into late week. PW values begin to decrease, but there\nwill still be sufficient moisture with the passing front to \nproduce scattered to numerous showers and storms into Thursday \n(rain chances up to 50-70%). Rain chances begin to decrease into \nFriday as drier air continues to filter into the area, with best \npotential for showers and storms (PoPs 30-50%) focused along the \ncoast and inland southeast of the I-4 corridor. \n\nTemperatures only slightly decrease as front moves through into late \nweek, with highs still reaching the mid to upper 80s for most \nlocations (except low 80s along the Volusia coast Friday). \nOvernight lows continue in the 70s Thursday night, but may start \nto see min temps in the upper 60s creep in across the interior \ninto Friday night.\n\nSaturday-Monday...A slight taste of Fall forecast into the weekend \nand early next week as drier air continues to filter into the area, \nleading to lowering rain chances and decreasing humidity. Highs will \nstill reach into the 80s, but dewpoints in the 60s will make for \nmore comfortable conditions. Lows in the mid to upper 60s forecast \nto be more widespread across the area, especially across the \ninterior. Breezy conditions will likely continue into Saturday, \nespecially along the coast, and then winds are forecast to decrease \nto around 10 to 15 mph Sunday into early next week.",
